= Thermodynamics — Technical =

== Closed-system first law ==
For a closed system with heat Q entering and work W done by the system, the internal-energy change is:

<pre>
ΔU = Q - W
</pre>

Q and W depend on the process path, while U is a state function. For open control volumes, mass-associated flows and other energy transfers must be included. The sign convention must be declared rather than assumed.

== Entropy and second law ==
For a reversible heat transfer at temperature T, a system's differential entropy change may be written dS = δQ_rev/T. In an isolated system, total entropy does not decrease; an individual subsystem's entropy can fall if surroundings gain enough entropy.

== Equilibrium and process models ==
Equation-of-state models relate variables such as pressure, volume and temperature for a specified material and regime. Quasi-static, reversible, isothermal and adiabatic describe different properties of a modeled process; none implies the others by definition.

== Thermodynamic versus Shannon entropy ==
The logarithmic structure of statistical-mechanical and information-theoretic entropy is related, but units and state spaces differ. Physical entropy statements require microscopic and thermodynamic assumptions, not just a probability histogram.

